      <h1>EB-2 National Interest Waiver: Can You Self-Petition?</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Yes, you can usually petition for yourself. The EB-2 national interest waiver removes the job offer and the labor certification, so no employer has to sponsor you. You still have to prove you qualify for EB-2, and you still need a visa number when your priority date arrives.</p>
<p>The waiver is not a separate visa category. It removes one requirement inside EB-2, and USCIS decides it on the same I-140 that determines whether you are EB-2 at all.</p>
<p>The harder question is usually not paperwork. It is whether you are strong enough to go without an employer. That is an evidence question, and a San Francisco <a href="/immigration-lawyer-san-francisco/">immigration lawyer</a> can assess it against the three prongs below.</p>
<h2 id="start-with-eb-2-eligibility-not-the-waiver">Start With EB-2 Eligibility, Not the Waiver</h2>
<p>Skip this step and everything after it is decoration. USCIS publishes two routes into EB-2, and you need one of them on paper before the national interest argument means anything. The <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/working-in-the-united-states/permanent-workers/employment-based-immigration-second-preference-eb-2">USCIS EB-2 page</a> is short and worth reading directly.</p>
<p><strong>Advanced degree.</strong> A master's or higher, or a bachelor's plus at least five years of progressive experience in the specialty. The degree must be a U.S. academic or professional degree, or a foreign degree evaluated as its equivalent. A three-year bachelor's raises an equivalency question that needs a credential evaluation.</p>
<p><strong>Exceptional ability.</strong> Expertise significantly above what is ordinarily encountered in your field. USCIS requires at least three of six criteria, and meeting three is only the first step: an academic record, letters documenting ten years of full-time experience, a license to practice, a salary reflecting exceptional ability, association membership, and recognition from peers or government bodies.</p>
<p>Clear neither route and the conversation stops. No amount of national-interest writing repairs a missing credential.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-eb-2-national-interest-waiver-removes">What the EB-2 National Interest Waiver Removes</h2>
<p>A standard EB-2 needs an employer with a job offer. Before filing, that employer must test the U.S. labor market through the Department of Labor, a step called labor certification. It starts with a prevailing wage determination, then recruitment, then a certification showing that no able, willing, and qualified U.S. worker was available. Only then does the I-140 go in.</p>
<p>The waiver, at INA § 203(b)(2)(B)(i), removes that stage. You file for yourself, with no job offer and no labor market test.</p>
<p>What it does <strong>not</strong> remove:</p>
<ul><li>The EB-2 standard. You still need the degree or the exceptional ability record.</li><li>Admissibility. Criminal history, health grounds, and prior violations follow their own rules.</li><li>The visa number. EB-2 is capped each year and capped again per country of birth.</li></ul>
<p>So the waiver buys independence. It does not buy a place at the front of the line.</p>
<h2 id="the-three-prong-test-in-plain-english">The Three-Prong Test, in Plain English</h2>
<p>The old framework came from <em>Matter of New York State Department of Transportation</em>. In 2016 the Administrative Appeals Office replaced it in <em>Matter of Dhanasar</em>, 26 I&amp;N Dec. 884: three prongs, taken in order rather than scored as one total.</p>
<p>Prongs one and two are threshold. USCIS reaches the balance test at prong three only after it has found both of them met. A strong third prong will not rescue a weak second. Build the case in that order and prong three becomes an argument you can actually win.</p>
<h3 id="prong-1-substantial-merit-and-national-importance">Prong 1: Substantial Merit and National Importance</h3>
<p>Merit is the easy half. National importance is where petitions get thin. USCIS asks whether the endeavor has impact beyond your employer, your clients, or your city.</p>
<p>National importance does not require a national footprint. Work in one region can qualify if it carries broader implications, like a public health program or water policy. Nor is it about prestige. A good title with no wider effect fails.</p>
<p>The other trap is describing a career instead of an endeavor. "I will continue working as a machine learning engineer" says nothing USCIS can evaluate. "I will build a model that cuts false positives in colorectal cancer screening" gives them something to weigh.</p>
<h3 id="prong-2-well-positioned-to-advance-the-endeavor">Prong 2: Well Positioned to Advance the Endeavor</h3>
<p>This prong is about execution, not achievement. USCIS looks at education, skills, record of success in related efforts, your plan, progress so far, and interest from customers or investors.</p>
<p>A long publication record in an unrelated subfield does not answer the question. Neither does a decade at a good company if none of it points at the work you now propose. Past success is evidence, not the standard. A plan with milestones beats a list of honors.</p>
<h3 id="prong-3-on-balance-waiving-the-labor-certification-benefits-the-united-states">Prong 3: On Balance, Waiving the Labor Certification Benefits the United States</h3>
<p>The question is whether the national interest is strong enough to justify skipping the labor market test Congress built into EB-2.</p>
<p>USCIS weighs whether labor certification would be impractical given the nature of your work, which can be a stronger argument for founders, self-employed researchers, and work that depends on one person's knowledge. It also weighs economic impact, job creation, and whether the interest is urgent. Shortage evidence in your occupation helps, but on its own it will not carry this prong.</p>
<p>The failure mode is subtle: a petition can be excellent on the first two prongs and still read as 'impressive, but nothing explains why the United States should give up the labor market test.' Useful beats impressive.</p>
<h2 id="evidence-that-moves-each-prong">Evidence That Moves Each Prong</h2>
<p>Generic exhibits get generic results. Build the file prong by prong.</p>
<p><strong>Prong one.</strong> A written endeavor statement, two or three paragraphs, naming the work and the problem it solves. Independent documentation that the field matters: agency priorities, government reports, industry standards.</p>
<p><strong>Prong two.</strong> Your CV and a short list of work directly related to the endeavor. Not everything you have ever done. Traction: pilots, contracts, letters of interest, grants.</p>
<p><strong>Prong three.</strong> Why labor certification does not fit. You are the founder. The knowledge is not transferable to another hire. Add shortage evidence and state the national consequence of delay.</p>
<p>Recommendation letters deserve a warning. They are the most overused exhibit in self-petitioned cases and often the weakest. A page from a program officer who can describe what your work changed beats five from famous names who have never met you.</p>
<h2 id="niw-vs-employer-sponsored-eb-2">NIW vs. Employer-Sponsored EB-2</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>EB-2 with labor certification</th><th>EB-2 national interest waiver</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who files the I-140</td><td>Your employer</td><td>You</td></tr><tr><td>Labor market test</td><td>Required, through the Department of Labor</td><td>Waived</td></tr><tr><td>Job offer</td><td>Required</td><td>Not required</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence focus</td><td>Recruitment, ability to pay</td><td>Endeavor, positioning, national interest</td></tr><tr><td>Visa number</td><td>Same queue</td><td>Same queue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="processing-two-clocks-not-one">Processing: Two Clocks, Not One</h2>
<p>Searches for "EB2 NIW processing time" return one number, and it describes only the first of two clocks.</p>
<p><strong>Clock one is the I-140.</strong> USCIS adjudicates the petition. Premium processing is available for this form and puts the case on a published business-day clock, with the premium fee refunded if the agency misses it. It does not touch the second clock.</p>
<p><strong>Clock two is the visa number.</strong> An approved I-140 is not a green card. EB-2 has annual limits and a per-country cap, and you cannot finish until a number is available for your category and country of birth. The State Department's Visa Bulletin tracks that queue and is republished monthly. Your priority date is the date USCIS received your I-140, and it holds your place in that queue. Dates move forward, and sometimes backward.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/working-in-the-united-states/permanent-workers">USCIS permanent workers page</a> describes the categories. For live numbers, use the USCIS processing times tool for the I-140 stage and the current Visa Bulletin for the queue.</p>
<p>When a number is available, you generally file Form I-485 to adjust status in the United States, and you may be able to file Form I-765 for work authorization and Form I-131 for advance parole while it pends. Abroad, the case goes through consular processing.</p>
<p>Anyone who quotes a total timeline without asking your country of birth is guessing. If your I-140 sits well past the posted time, that is a different problem, and our guide to <a href="/blog/uscis-immigration-case-delay/">USCIS case delays</a> covers it.</p>
<h2 id="who-should-not-file-an-niw">Who Should Not File an NIW</h2>
<ul><li><strong>You have a willing employer and no backlog problem.</strong> That route costs less and carries lower evidentiary risk.</li><li><strong>Your endeavor is your job description.</strong> If you will simply keep doing your current job well, the third prong has nothing to stand on.</li><li><strong>Your evidence is thin and your status clock is short.</strong> An RFE on a self-petition can burn months you do not have.</li><li><strong>You have an admissibility problem.</strong> The waiver does not cure unlawful presence, a prior removal order, or a criminal issue.</li><li><strong>Your plan and your life point in different directions.</strong> Take an unrelated corporate job next year and the petition describes a person who does not exist.</li></ul>
<p>There is also a case for waiting. Six more months of published work or one funded grant can turn a weak second prong into a strong one.</p>
<h2 id="a-decision-worth-getting-right">A Decision Worth Getting Right</h2>
<p>A green card petition is a legal argument with evidence attached. The NIW version is more exposed than most, because no employer stands behind it to absorb a mistake.</p>
<p>A real assessment looks like this. Someone reads your credentials against the EB-2 definitions before discussing strategy. Someone asks what your work changes, not what it is called.</p>
